[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
```js
// Mapbox GL JS (v5) – default export
// import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl';

// MapLibre GL JS (v6) – no default export
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';          // namespace import
// or, import only what you need
import { Map, NavigationControl } from 'maplibre-gl';
```

*Why the change?*  
Starting with v6, **MapLibre GL JS no longer provides a default export**. Use a namespace import (`* as maplibregl`) or import the specific symbols you need (`Map`, `NavigationControl`, etc.). The rest of your code can stay the same, just replace `mapboxgl` with `maplibregl` (or the named symbols you imported).

**Important note – `require('maplibre-gl')` no longer works in v6**  
Starting with MapLibre GL JS v6 the package ships **ESM-only**. Its `package.json` has an `"import"` entry but no `"require"` entry, so a plain

```js
const maplibregl = require('maplibregl-gl');
```

throws `ERR_PACKAGE_PATH_NOT_EXPORTED`.  
If you must keep a CommonJS file you have two options:

1. **Load the module dynamically with `import()`** (still a CommonJS file, but the actual load is ESM).  
2. **Pin the older v5 release** (`npm i maplibre-gl@5`) – it still supports `require()`.

Below is the recommended CommonJS-style script that works with the current v6 release by using a dynamic `import()`. It prints the library’s version (exposed as `maplibregl.version`).

```js
// print-maplibre-version.cjs
// ───────────────────────
// This file is a CommonJS module (extension .cjs or "type": "commonjs" in package.json)
// It uses a dynamic import to load the ESM-only maplibre-gl package.

(async () => {
  try {
    // Dynamically import the ESM build. No default export – we get a namespace object.
    const maplibregl = await import('maplibre-gl');

    // The version string is exported as `version` (same as in v5).
    console.log('MapLibre GL version:', maplibregl.version);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Failed to load maplibre-gl:', err);
    process.exit(1);
  }
})();
```

### How to run it

```bash
# Install the latest v6 release (or a specific version you need)
npm install maplibre-gl@^6.0.0

# Run the script
node print-maplibre-version.cjs
```

You should see something like:

```
MapLibre GL version: 6.13.0
```

---

#### If you really need a synchronous `require()` call

```bash
npm install maplibre-gl@5   # v5 still ships a CommonJS bundle
```

Then the classic script works:

```js
// v5-only, synchronous CommonJS
const maplibregl = require('maplibre-gl');
console.log('MapLibre GL version:', maplibregl.version);
```

But for any new development on v6, stick with the dynamic-import pattern shown first.

### Supplying a missing icon at runtime in MapLibre GL JS v6

When a style references an `icon-image` name that isn’t present in the sprite sheet, MapLibre now **notifies** you via the `styleimagemissing` event **but does not resolve the request automatically**.  
To actually provide the image you have to register a *missing-style-image resolver* with `map.setMissingStyleImageResolver()`.  
The resolver receives the missing image id, lets you load or generate the image, and you add it to the map with `map.addImage()`. Once the image is added, MapLibre will re-evaluate the style and the icon will appear.

#### 1. Register the resolver (once, before the map is created or as early as possible)

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

// Create the map first – the resolver can be set after construction
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://example.com/your-style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2,
});

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Register a resolver for missing style images
// ---------------------------------------------------
map.setMissingStyleImageResolver(async (missingId) => {
  try {
    // ----> YOUR custom loading / generation logic <----
    // Example 1: fetch an SVG from a CDN and turn it into an ImageBitmap
    const response = await fetch(`https://my-icons.example.com/${missingId}.svg`);
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error('icon not found');

    const svgText = await response.text();
    const blob = new Blob([svgText], { type: 'image/svg+xml' });
    const bitmap = await createImageBitmap(blob);

    // Example 2: generate a canvas-based icon on the fly
    // const bitmap = await generateCanvasIcon(missingId);

    // ---------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣  Add the image to the map (must be called inside the resolver)
    // ---------------------------------------------------
    map.addImage(missingId, bitmap, { sdf: true }); // `sdf` if you need tinting
  } catch (err) {
    console.warn(`Could not load missing icon "${missingId}":`, err);
    // Optional: add a fallback placeholder so the style doesn’t stay empty
    const placeholder = new ImageData(1, 1); // 1×1 transparent pixel
    map.addImage(missingId, placeholder);
  }
});
```

#### 2. What the resolver receives

* `missingId: string` – the exact value that appeared in the `icon-image` property of the style (e.g. `"my-custom-pin"`).

The resolver **must** add the image with the same id (`map.addImage(missingId, …)`).  
The return value of the resolver is ignored; you don’t need to `return` anything.

#### 3. Optional: listen to the notification-only event

If you only need to know *that* an image is missing (e.g. for logging) you can still attach a listener:

```js
map.on('styleimagemissing', (e) => {
  console.info('Missing style image:', e.id);
});
```

But **do not** try to resolve the image from this handler – it will no longer work in v6.

#### 4. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Dynamic missing-icon demo</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { position: absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';

  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        osm: { type: 'raster', tiles: ['https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'], tileSize: 256 }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'raster',
          type: 'raster',
          source: 'osm'
        },
        {
          id: 'points',
          type: 'symbol',
          source: 'osm',
          layout: {
            // This icon does NOT exist in the sprite → triggers resolver
            'icon-image': 'dynamic-pin',
            'icon-size': 1.5
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // Register the resolver (same code as in the snippet above)
  map.setMissingStyleImageResolver(async (id) => {
    // Simple example: generate a red circle on a canvas
    const size = 64;
    const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
    canvas.width = canvas.height = size;
    const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
    ctx.fillStyle = 'red';
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.arc(size / 2, size / 2, size / 2 - 2, 0, Math.PI * 2);
    ctx.fill();

    const bitmap = await createImageBitmap(canvas);
    map.addImage(id, bitmap);
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

When the map loads, the `icon-image: 'dynamic-pin'` isn’t in the sprite, the resolver runs, creates a red-circle icon, adds it, and the point appears.

---

## Quick checklist

| ✅ What you need to do | ❌ What **won’t** work in v6 |
|------------------------|------------------------------|
| Call `map.setMissingStyleImageResolver()` **once** (before the first missing-icon request). | Expect `map.on('styleimagemissing', …)` to resolve the request by calling `map.addImage()` inside the handler. |
| Inside the resolver, load/generate the image **and** call `map.addImage(missingId, image, options?)`. | Return an `ImageBitmap` or `HTMLImageElement` from the handler – the return value is ignored. |
| Use any async technique (`fetch`, `createImageBitmap`, canvas, etc.) – the resolver can be `async`. | Use synchronous `map.addImage()` from a `styleimagemissing` listener only for side-effects (e.g., logging). |
| Keep the same `id` you received (`missingId`). | Change the id when adding – MapLibre will still think the image is missing. |

---

### TL;DR

```js
map.setMissingStyleImageResolver(async (id) => {
  const img = await loadOrCreateImage(id);   // your custom logic
  map.addImage(id, img, { sdf: true });      // now the style can use it
});
```

That’s the modern, v6-compatible way to supply icons on-the-fly when they’re not present in the style’s sprite. Happy mapping!

```js
// ESM import – v6 has no default export
import {Map, NavigationControl} from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

// -------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Create the map
// -------------------------------------------------------------------
const map = new Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div>
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],                 // initial lon/lat
  zoom: 2,
  bearing: 0,
  pitch: 0,
});

// -------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Public getters give you the camera state you need
// -------------------------------------------------------------------
function logCameraState() {
  const center = map.getCenter();          // → LngLat object
  const zoom   = map.getZoom();            // → number
  const bearing = map.getBearing();        // → number (degrees)
  const pitch   = map.getPitch();          // → number (degrees)

  console.log({
    lng: center.lng,
    lat: center.lat,
    zoom,
    bearing,
    pitch,
  });
}

// Call it whenever you like (e.g. after the map is ready)
map.once('load', logCameraState);
map.on('move', logCameraState);   // updates on pan/zoom/rotate


// -------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Getting the projection matrix
// -------------------------------------------------------------------
// The raw projection matrix is no longer exposed on `map.transform`.
// The only public place where MapLibre hands you that matrix is
// inside a *custom layer*’s `render` callback.
//
// A custom layer receives a `gl` context and a `projectionData`
// object that contains the matrix (`projectionData.projectionMatrix`).
// You can store it somewhere, call a callback, or do your math
// directly inside `render`.

const myMathLayer = {
  id: 'my-math-layer',
  type: 'custom',
  // The layer must implement `render` (and optionally `onAdd`/`onRemove`).
  render: (gl, matrix) => {
    // `matrix` is a 4×4 column-major Float32Array that MapLibre uses
    // for the current view-projection.  It already incorporates
    // center, zoom, bearing, pitch and the chosen projection (WebMercator
    // by default).

    // Example: log the matrix (or feed it to your own shader / math)
    console.log('Projection matrix:', matrix);

    // If you need the matrix elsewhere, you can copy it:
    // const projMatrix = new Float32Array(matrix);
    // myExternalFunction(projMatrix);
  },
};

map.addLayer(myMathLayer);
```

### What the code does

| Piece | How you obtain it |
|------|-------------------|
| **Center** | `map.getCenter()` → `{lng, lat}` |
| **Zoom**   | `map.getZoom()` |
| **Bearing**| `map.getBearing()` (degrees) |
| **Pitch**  | `map.getPitch()` (degrees) |
| **Projection matrix** | Inside a custom layer’s `render(gl, matrix)` callback; `matrix` is the current view-projection matrix. |

> **Why the matrix isn’t a top-level getter** – In v6 the internal `map.transform` object was removed. The only supported public entry point for the matrix is the custom-layer render API. If you need the matrix outside a custom layer, capture it there (e.g., store it in a variable or call a callback) and use it elsewhere in your code.

```ts
import { Map } from 'maplibre-gl';
import type {
  MapSourceDataEvent,
  MapStyleDataEvent,
} from 'maplibre-gl';

// Helper type-guard – narrows to a source-data event
function isSourceDataEvent(
  e: MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent,
): e is MapSourceDataEvent {
  return 'sourceDataType' in e;
}

// Example map creation
const map = new Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2,
});

// Type-safe “data” handler
map.on('data', (e: MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent) => {
  if (isSourceDataEvent(e)) {
    // e is now a MapSourceDataEvent – you can safely read sourceDataType
    switch (e.sourceDataType) {
      case 'metadata':
        console.log('Source metadata loaded for', e.sourceId);
        break;
      case 'source':
        console.log('Source data ready for', e.sourceId);
        break;
      case 'tile':
        console.log(
          `Tile ${e.tile?.z}/${e.tile?.x}/${e.tile?.y} loaded for source ${e.sourceId}`,
        );
        break;
      // add other sourceDataType cases as needed
    }
  } else {
    // e is a MapStyleDataEvent – style-related data event
    console.log('Style data event:', e);
  }
});
```

Below is a minimal, **v6-compatible** way to get a MapLibre GL JS map running in a Vite + TypeScript project that you installed from npm (`npm i maplibre-gl`).  
All the required steps are highlighted in the comments.

```ts
// src/main.ts --------------------------------------------------------------
import { Map, setWorkerUrl } from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';               // <-- map CSS
// Vite’s special “?worker&url” query gives you the URL of the worker file
import workerUrl from 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?worker&url';

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣ Tell MapLibre where its Web-Worker lives (required for bundlers)
//    Do this **once**, before any Map instance is created.
setWorkerUrl(workerUrl);

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣ Create the map – you can use the named imports or a namespace import
//    (both work, but the named import keeps the bundle a bit smaller).
const map = new Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div id="map"></div> in index.html
  style:    'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any valid style URL
  center:   [0, 0],               // [lng, lat]
  zoom:     2,
  // optional: you can still pass any other Map options here
});

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣ (Optional) add controls, listeners, etc.
import { NavigationControl } from 'maplibre-gl';
map.addControl(new NavigationControl());

// Example of a typed “data” listener – note the new event types
import type {
  MapSourceDataEvent,
  MapStyleDataEvent,
} from 'maplibre-gl';

map.on('data', (e: MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent) => {
  if ('sourceId' in e) {
    // Source-related data event
    console.log('source data:', e.sourceId, e.sourceDataType);
  } else {
    // Style-related data event
    console.log('style data loaded');
  }
});
```

### What you need in the HTML

```html
<!-- public/index.html ---------------------------------------------------- -->
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <title>Vite + MapLibre GL JS</title>
  <style>
    /* make the map fill the viewport */
    html, body, #map { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div id="map"></div>
  <script type="module" src="/src/main.ts"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Vite config (no special changes needed)

A plain Vite config works out-of-the-box because the `?worker&url` import already resolves the worker file for you.

```ts
// vite.config.ts -----------------------------------------------------------
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'; // if you use React, otherwise omit

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [react()], // optional
});
```

### Why these steps are required after the v5 → v6 upgrade

| v5 pattern | v6 change | What you do in the snippet |
|------------|-----------|----------------------------|
| `import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'` (default export) | **Default export removed** | Use named/namespace import (`import { Map } …` or `import * as maplibregl …`). |
| No worker URL handling | **Bundlers must call `setWorkerUrl()`** | `setWorkerUrl(workerUrl)` with the Vite-specific `?worker&url` import. |
| `map.on('data', (e) => …)` typed as `MapDataEvent` | **`MapDataEvent` removed** | Type the handler as `MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent` (or just `any` if you don’t need typing). |
| `require('maplibre-gl')` | **CommonJS no longer supported** | Use ESM `import` (as shown). |
| CDN script tag without `type="module"` | **Only ESM build exists** | Not applicable here – you’re using npm + Vite. |

That’s it! Run `npm run dev` (or `vite`) and you should see a working MapLibre map. Adjust the `style`, `center`, `zoom`, or add your own sources/layers as needed.

Below is a minimal, **v6-only** example that shows how to:

1. Load a **PMTiles** file (a single-file container for vector tiles) with the official `pmtiles` library.  
2. Register the `pmtiles://` protocol so MapLibre can request tiles from the file.  
3. Add the vector source to the map.  
4. Create a simple **line** layer that draws the features from that source.

> **Prerequisites** – you need a recent build of MapLibre GL JS v6 (≥ 6.0.0) and the `pmtiles` package.

```bash
# npm / yarn / pnpm
npm i maplibre-gl@^6 pmtiles
# or
yarn add maplibre-gl@^6 pmtiles
```

---

## 1️⃣  Import the libraries (ESM)

```js
// main.js – an ES module (type="module" in HTML or .mjs file)
import { Map, setWorkerUrl } from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';   // optional, but keeps the default UI
import { PMTiles, registerProtocol } from 'pmtiles';

// If you are bundling (Vite, webpack, etc.) you must tell MapLibre where its worker lives:
import workerUrl from 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?worker&url';
setWorkerUrl(workerUrl);
```

> **Why the `setWorkerUrl` call?**  
> In a bundler environment the worker cannot be auto-discovered, so we give it the URL that the bundler emits.  
> If you are loading MapLibre directly from a CDN with `<script type="module">`, you can skip the `setWorkerUrl` part.

---

## 2️⃣  Register the `pmtiles://` protocol

```js
// Path (or URL) to the local PMTiles file. It can be a relative URL, an absolute URL,
/// or a Blob URL if you obtained the file via a file-input element.
const pmtilesPath = '/data/my-tiles.pmtiles';

// Create the PMTiles object and register the custom protocol.
const pmtiles = new PMTiles(pmtilesPath);
registerProtocol(pmtiles);
```

After this registration, any MapLibre source that uses a URL beginning with `pmtiles://` will be resolved by the `pmtiles` library.

---

## 3️⃣  Create the map

```js
const map = new Map({
  container: 'map',               // <div id="map"></div> in your HTML
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style you like
  center: [0, 0],                 // lon, lat
  zoom: 2,
});
```

---

## 4️⃣  Add the vector-tile source that points at the PMTiles file

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  // The source name can be anything you want.
  map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
    type: 'vector',
    // The `url` uses the custom protocol we registered above.
    // `{z}/{x}/{y}` are the usual tile placeholders.
    url: 'pmtiles://my-pmtiles-source/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf',
    // Optional: if your PMTiles file contains multiple layers you can
    // limit the source to a specific layer name (or keep it open for all).
    // Example: `layers: ['roads']`
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣  Add a line layer that draws from that source
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'my-lines',
    type: 'line',
    source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
    // The name of the vector layer inside the PMTiles file.
    // You can inspect it with `pmtiles info my-tiles.pmtiles` or any
    // MBTiles/PMTiles viewer.
    'source-layer': 'roads',          // <-- change to the actual layer name
    layout: {
      'line-cap': 'round',
      'line-join': 'round',
    },
    paint: {
      // Simple styling – a thick, semi-transparent blue line.
      'line-color': '#1E90FF',
      'line-width': [
        'interpolate',
        ['linear'],
        ['zoom'],
        5, 1,
        12, 4,
        18, 12,
      ],
      'line-opacity': 0.8,
    },
  });
});
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **`map.addSource`** | Declares a *vector* source whose tiles are fetched via the `pmtiles://` protocol. The `{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf` placeholders are automatically replaced by MapLibre when it requests a tile. |
| **`source-layer`** | The name of the layer inside the vector tile set (e.g., `roads`, `railways`). You must use the exact name that exists in the PMTiles file. |
| **`layout` / `paint`** | Standard MapLibre line-layer styling. The example uses an interpolated width that grows with zoom. Feel free to adjust colors, dash arrays, etc. |

---

## 6️⃣  Full HTML skeleton (for quick testing)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre + PMTiles line demo</title>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div id="map"></div>

  <!-- ESM-only CDN version (no bundler) -->
  <script type="module">
    import { Map, setWorkerUrl } from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';
    import 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6/dist/maplibre-gl.css';
    import { PMTiles, registerProtocol } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@latest/dist/index.mjs';

    // (Optional) CDN workers are auto-detected, so we skip setWorkerUrl here.

    // Register the PMTiles file (adjust the path to your own file)
    const pmtiles = new PMTiles('/data/my-tiles.pmtiles');
    registerProtocol(pmtiles);

    const map = new Map({
      container: 'map',
      style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
      center: [0, 0],
      zoom: 2,
    });

    map.on('load', () => {
      map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
        type: 'vector',
        url: 'pmtiles://my-pmtiles-source/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf',
      });

      map.addLayer({
        id: 'my-lines',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
        'source-layer': 'roads',   // <-- change to your layer name
        layout: { 'line-cap': 'round', 'line-join': 'round' },
        paint: {
          'line-color': '#1E90FF',
          'line-width': [
            'interpolate',
            ['linear'],
            ['zoom'],
            5, 1,
            12, 4,
            18, 12,
          ],
          'line-opacity': 0.8,
        },
      });
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

